atc -- Air Traffic Controller=============================    atc -g easyThe radar shows the arena: exits numbered round the edge, airports as anumber with the direction planes land and take off (^ > < v), beacons *,and planes as a letter and an altitude -- capitals are props, lower casejets.  Beside it the list of planes and where each is bound, and the clockwith the count of planes you have brought in safely.Talk to a plane by its letter.  `a' and a digit sets its altitude inthousands of feet, and at an airport that is the take-off; `t' turns it,`tl' and `tr' by 45 degrees or to a direction key (wedcxzaq around s);`c' circles.  RETURN sends the command and `?' lists what may come next.A plane must leave by its own exit at 9000 feet or land at its ownairport, heading the right way at altitude 0.  Running out of fuel, acollision, or the wrong exit ends the game.  ^L redraws; ^C quits.`atc -l' lists the airports: default, easy, crossover, Killer, game_2.Scores are kept in GAMES/ATC/atc_score; `atc -s' prints them.By Ed James, UC Berkeley, as 4.3BSD Net/2 carried it, from The UnixHeritage Society's archive.  Full originals in SRC/atc/ORIG.